  "method": "Batch of 646 witnesses in 24 problems (`MANIFEST.json` + `records/<record_id>.json` + `SUMMARY.tsv`), each below the current `records/SUMMARY.csv` entry (main at b99b2d88, checked 2026-11-21). Every record passes `verify_general.py` (VALID) and `certify_general.py` (CERTIFIED) from that commit locally, run on the files extracted from the archive. The changes against the current records: median -0.00 %, largest -2.42 %; 10 records improve by \u2265 1 %, 33 by \u2265 0.1 %.\n\n**Relation to earlier submissions.** the public records were rebuilt after #136 and #143 were merged (main b99b2d88); every record here is below the value in that archive and below the values in open submissions.\n\n**Against PolyPack.** I also compared with PolyPack's published records (https://github.com/sinkomr/PolyPack, `data/polyhedra/records.json`, fetched 2026-11-21) and kept only records that are below both the archive and PolyPack (260 of these records have a PolyPack value; `SUMMARY.tsv` lists both values, `polypack_s`, `change_vs_best`).\n\n**How the records were made.** `found_by`, `credit` and `method` identify where each record starts; `method` or `refinement` gives the steps.\n\n- *Refinements of catalogue records* (426): a contact-preserving descent (sequential linear programming with the contacts as inequalities) started from the catalogue record named in `refinement` (for some, a randomised multi-start search from perturbed copies of that record comes first; `refinement` says so). `found_by`, `credit` and `method` are the catalogue record's own, copied verbatim; my part is in `refined_by` and `refinement_credit`. For 5 of them the start was the record for a different n and the first n pieces were kept (`derived_from`). Rigidity analysis and code: https://doi.org/10.5281/zenodo.23630728\n- *Layer-by-row and lattice constructions* (4): structured constructions with all pieces in one orientation. For cubes, the pieces are stacked in parallel layers and each layer is f